      "englishText": "Aprastutaprasanga: Respected Avadhānī, why do you express everything through feminine gender? Has my sister (the Avadhānī's wife) appeared before your mind?\n\nAvadhānī: If your sister comes to my mind there is no harm, —but if anyone else does, then it may create problems.\n\nAprastutaprasanga: I shall inform her then, if there is anyone else.\n\nAvadhānī: (quotes from the scriptures)\n\nprātaḥ dyutaprasangena madhyāhne striprasangatah/\nrātrau cauraprasangena kālo gacchati dhīmatām//\n(The wise spend their mornings in chatting about the game of dice, the topic of woman is\n\ntheirs at noon, at night they indulge in talks about thieves)19\n\nThis is how it is said in Sanskrit. Since our programme has started at noontime, the topic 'woman' is still continuing. \n\nVarṇanā: Indeed, Poetry is your second wife.\n\nAvadhānī: (completes now the last pāda of the Eshu)\n\n...sapadi mahita jhañjhā vyākulāḥ smāraṇīyāḥ//\n\n(suddenly grew victim to destruction through the storm's might. This is worth-remembering.)\n\n19Traditionally in India Mahabharata is read in the morning, the Ramayana, in the noon and the Bhagavata, in the night. The game of dice here refers to Mahabharata. Topic of woman refers to Ramayana. Talks about a thief refers to Bhagavata.",
      "sanskritDevanagari": "अप्रस्तुतप्रसङ्गः:  अवधानिवर्य! किं सर्वदा स्त्रीप्रसङ्ग एव प्रचयति ।   किं मम भगिनी अत्रा स्मृतिपथमारोहति वा ।\n\nअवधानी:  भगिनी स्मृतिपथमारोहति त£ह चिन्ता न भवति  ।  किन्तु भवतः अन्यः स्मृतिपथमाराहति त£ह---   कÎम् ।\n\nअप्रस्तुतप्रसङ्गः:  त£ह अन्या अपि  अस्ति त£ह सूच;ष्यामि ।\n\nअवधानी:  प्रातः |ुतप्रसङ्गेन मध्याह्ने स्त्रीप्रसङ्गतः ।\nरात्रौ चौरप्रसङ्गेन कालो गच्छति धीमताम् ।।\nइति एकमाभाणकमस्ति ।  अतः रामस्य चरितमेव रामायाः चरितम् ।  अतः मध्याह्ने प्रारब्धमिति कृत्वा सा एव अनुवर्तते ।  न समाप्त इति ।\n\nवर्णना  भवतामपरा ýी कविता एव ।\n\nअवधानी:  ---सपदि महितण्×छाल्यावुðयाः स्मारणीयाः ।।"
